{
 "id": "2018-moura-seven",
 "name": "Seven Biomes: Landscape Preference and Emotions (Moura et al. 2018)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Moura, J. M. B., Ferreira Júnior, W. S., Silva, T. C., & Albuquerque, U. P. (2018). The Influence of the Evolutionary Past on the Mind: An Analysis of the Preference for Landscapes in the Human Species. Frontiers in Psychology 9:2485. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2018.02485\n\nDoes everyone prefer savanna? Part 1: each participant sees ONE photograph drawn at random from 14 and rates six emotions on 5–1 semantic-differential pairs (pleasure–unpleasure, enthusiasm–discouragement, happiness–sadness, freedom–imprisonment, safety–unsafety, interest–disinterest; Appendix A). Part 2: all 14 photographs (two each of savanna, tundra, urban, deciduous seasonal forest, coniferous forest, desert and rainforest) rated from 10 \"I liked it a lot\" to 1 \"I did not like it at all\". Analysis as in the paper: sum of the six emotions (6–30) and preference medians per landscape type, Kruskal–Wallis with Dunn post hoc, by living environment. In the original study (50 Caatinga residents, 82 Atlantic-forest residents, 189 urban students in Pernambuco, Brazil) rainforest was preferred in all three groups.\n\nPhotographs: the 14 images printed in the paper's Figure 2 (CC BY 4.0; the authors took them from Pixabay and only evened out sky colour), cropped from the figure at about 340 × 200 px with the panel letters removed. Credited to the authors; the template is removed if they object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online instead of printed 23 × 19 cm photographs shown by a researcher; (2) the 14 photographs appear one at a time in a new random order per participant (the paper used one fixed, randomly drawn order that it does not report); (3) photographs are lower resolution, cut from the published figure; (4) the consent line and the about-you questions (gender, age group, living environment) are written for this template — the paper recruited by place of residence rather than asking.",
